            <ArticleTitle>Eosinophilic activity and bronchial hyperresponsiveness within an asthmatic paediatric population.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>301-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.aller.2011.06.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0301-0546(11)00255-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Our study aims to assess the importance of serum eosinophil cationic protein (ECP) levels as a non-invasive marker of bronchial hyperresponsiveness (BHR) in children with asthma, and may predict objectively the asthmatic severity and sensitivities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This study, which was carried out on 75 asthmatic patients from a paediatric population (average age: nine years old, sex-ratio M/F: 1.64), is based on both interrogation conducted by the clinician and biological explorations, essentially serological testing of ECP and eosinophilia determination, as well as the measurement of serological IgE amounts.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The analysis of the questionnaires and the biological results allowed us to evaluate the clinico-biological relations within this population. ECP, more than eosinophilia, proves to be a relevant marker of asthma severity (p&lt;0.05) and sensitivities within this given population (r=0.65).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We were able to show that the evaluation of the serological levels of ECP seems to be a good biological marker of asthma.</AbstractText>
